The two detection approaches from this lesson correspond to two branches of the same function, depending on whether retrieved context is available:

```python
def detect_hallucination(answer_claims, retrieved_docs=None, repeated_answers=None):
    """Illustrates the two detection approaches from this lesson:
    grounded claim-checking when retrieved context exists, and
    self-consistency checking when it doesn't."""
    if retrieved_docs is not None:
        # Grounded case: does each claim appear supported by retrieved text?
        unsupported = [
            claim for claim in answer_claims
            if not any(claim.lower() in doc.lower() for doc in retrieved_docs)
        ]
        return {
            "mode": "grounded",
            "unsupported_claims": unsupported,
            "hallucination_rate": len(unsupported) / len(answer_claims) if answer_claims else 0,
        }

    # Ungrounded case: ask the same question multiple times and compare.
    if repeated_answers:
        unique_answers = set(repeated_answers)
        consistency = 1 - (len(unique_answers) - 1) / len(repeated_answers)
        return {
            "mode": "self_consistency",
            "consistency_score": consistency,
            "likely_hallucinating": consistency < 0.5,
        }

    return {"mode": "unknown", "error": "no context or repeated samples provided"}

claims = ["The refund window is 30 days", "Shipping is free over $50"]
docs = ["Our refund window is 30 days from purchase."]
print(detect_hallucination(claims, retrieved_docs=docs))
# flags the free-shipping claim as unsupported by the retrieved context
```

The grounded branch is the RAG-specific claim-checking approach; the self-consistency branch is the fallback for ungrounded generation where no retrieved reference exists to check against.
